This complete standalone JavaScript adapter is memory-only practice:
```javascript
const store = {
  rows: [],
  save(row) { this.rows.push({ ...row }); },
  load() { return this.rows.map(row => ({ ...row })); }
};
function saveNote(row) {
  store.save(row);
}
function loadNotes() {
  return store.load();
}
saveNote({ id: 'x', minutes: 35 });
saveNote({ id: 'y', minutes: 12 });
console.log(loadNotes());
```
It returns both rows. this means the store object inside its methods. {...row} copies the flat record, and map returns a new array of copied records. Saving the second row appends rather than replacing the first.

Save this complete example as storage.mjs and run it with Node.js 22.13 or newer, whose node:sqlite module is available in this lesson's required environment:
```javascript
import { DatabaseSync } from 'node:sqlite';
const db = new DatabaseSync('demo-practice.sqlite');
db.exec('CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS notes(id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, minutes INTEGER NOT NULL)');
const insert = db.prepare('INSERT INTO notes(id, minutes) VALUES(?, ?)');
insert.run('x', 35);
insert.run('y', 12);
const saved = db.prepare('SELECT id, minutes FROM notes ORDER BY id').all();
console.log(saved);
db.close();
```
A first run in a folder without that file prints x/35 and y/12 and leaves a database file. db.prepare builds a statement; run executes the insert with two values; all returns the query's rows. This demonstration should be inserted only once: rerunning those same primary keys causes a duplicate-key error.

To verify persistence, use a separate reader after the first script has exited:
```javascript
import { DatabaseSync } from 'node:sqlite';
const db = new DatabaseSync('demo-practice.sqlite');
console.log(db.prepare('SELECT id, minutes FROM notes ORDER BY id').all());
db.close();
```
Save as read-storage.mjs in the same folder and run it. The same rows still exist because the file outlived the first process. A different working folder can open a different file with the same relative name, so check your location when testing persistence.

Set membership helps compare ids. Suppose saved ids are {x, y} and requested ids are {x, z}. Their overlap is {x}, because only x occurs in both. z is requested but missing, and y is stored but not requested. Positions do not decide membership. A JavaScript Set stores distinct values and has(id) checks whether a particular id is present.

```javascript
const savedIds = new Set(["x", "y"]);
console.log(savedIds.has("x"));
console.log(savedIds.has("z"));
```
This prints true, then false. has receives one candidate value, returns a Boolean and does not change the set.

